```java
public interface Plugin {
    String name();
    void init(PluginContext ctx);
    void execute(Request req, Response res);
    void shutdown();
}

public class PluginRegistry {
    private final List<Plugin> plugins = new ArrayList<>();
    public void register(Plugin p) { plugins.add(p); }
    public List<Plugin> all() { return List.copyOf(plugins); }
}
```

`PluginContext` is the one thing worth designing carefully — it's the narrow surface the host exposes inward, deliberately smaller than the host's full internal API, so a plugin can't reach into things it shouldn't.

```java
public void execute(Request req, Response res) {
    for (Plugin p : plugins.all()) {
        try {
            runWithTimeout(() -> p.execute(req, res), Duration.ofMillis(200));
        } catch (Exception | TimeoutException e) {
            log.warn("plugin {} failed", p.name(), e);
        }
    }
}
```

Every plugin call is wrapped individually — one plugin throwing, hanging, or timing out only removes that plugin's contribution for this request; it never propagates up and takes down the host or blocks the other plugins.
